\section{Data processing and model families}
\label{app:families}

Models repeated under the same name in the Open LLM Leaderboard files are dropped (the first is kept), and so is any model whose response vector is identical to that of an earlier model.
Items that every model answers identically are kept; they add no variance and are counted in Table~\ref{tab:audit}.
For the HELM files the model of each row comes from the list of 83 models published with the data.
The \texttt{legalbench} file has 79 rows and no published mapping from rows to models, so its families are unknown: each row is its own family, the file is left out of the lineage split, and its intervals are clustered by row.

A model's family is the first keyword (for example \texttt{qwen}, \texttt{llama}, \texttt{mistral}, \texttt{claude}) found in its lower-cased name, and otherwise the organisation prefix of the name.
The rule is a heuristic and was checked by reading the resulting groups; families may still merge unrelated models or split related ones.
All intervals resample whole families, with every subset draw of a family kept together.

